  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nTwo MCSs are ongoing as of 11 AM, one in eastern Kansas and the\nother in eastern Oklahoma. The system in eastern Oklahoma is\nlikely to weaken/dip south and miss the Mid-South, but the Kansas\nsystem track is likely to skirt across the region. Showers and\nthunderstorms threatening damaging winds and large hail will\naccompany this system this afternoon and evening. Inverted-V\nmodel soundings increase confidence in the damaging wind threat.\nMid-level lapse rates around 7C/km, combined with a bit of dry\nair, will support hail growth as well, which is well represented\nby the Slight Risk (level 2/5) for severe weather. Much of the\n12z CAM guidance favors this afternoon between 3-10 PM as the\nmost likely timing for severe thunderstorm development.\n\nAs northwest flow aloft continues, the Mid-South is susceptible\nto systems threatening damaging wind and hail each day through at\nleast midweek. Timing and placement will be refined as convective\nevolution becomes clearer. Due to the potential for storms each\nday, this does keep temperatures on the 'cooler side' with\nseasonal temperatures (low to mid 90s) instead of the extreme\nheat we have experienced thus far. Probabilities for heat index\nvalues above 105F are generally less than 20% through the work\nweek, with some local hot spots in the Delta Region. Confidence\nis highest in this pattern sticking around through at least\nWednesday. Confidence in the synoptic pattern decreases\nsignificantly beyond Wednesday due to model divergence. Be sure\nto monitor the forecast this week, especially if you have outdoor\nafternoon plans!\n\nDNM",
